---
title: The hot path
---
Everything close to the best route through a map: the part a player is likely to see [[t:257-262 s:28]].

## The rule
```text
find the route from the start to the exit
flood outward from every tile of the route at once, counting steps
tiles under a chosen count are the hot path
optionally, turn every tile beyond a second count to wall
```

The talk’s author finds the route, makes a Dijkstra map from all of its points, and takes everything under a value of, say, 10 [[t:258-262]]. The demo then culls at 15, so a band of floor survives beyond the hot path it shows [[c20:140-152]].

## Step through it
@@steps limit=4

## Controls
@@control radius
Tiles fewer than this many steps from the route are the hot path [[c20:140-144]]. The lab’s walk also keeps its caches at least this far from the route [[own]].
@@control cull
Tiles this many steps or more from the route become wall [[c20:148-152]]. At 0 nothing is culled [[own]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

The cull at 15 keeps about half the floor: a share of {{c20.floor_share.median}} at the median against {{c18.floor_share.median}} before it, on a route of the same {{c20.path.median}} steps [[run:c20.floor_share.median c18.floor_share.median c20.path.median]]. The talk offers it for a less branchy game, one that doesn’t make the player explore winding labyrinths with no purpose [[t:263-266]].

